@* What a scenario adds to the difficulty, as the badge every surface that names one wears — the
   reference table, the picker card and the record sheet's header. The tone is
   GameLookups.DifficultyBadge's, which the three had each read for themselves, and the figure is
   spelled the one way: "+2", never "2" or "Difficulty +2" on one and bare on the next.

   Layout stays with the caller, as it does for every badge: the picker pins it to the far end of
   its card and the others let it sit in a row. *@

<span class="badge @GameLookups.DifficultyBadge(Difficulty) @Class">@Prefix+@Difficulty</span>

@code {
    /// <summary>What the scenario adds — <c>Scenario.Difficulty</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public int Difficulty { get; set; }

    /// <summary>A word before the figure, where the badge stands alone rather than in a column
    /// headed "Difficulty" — the record sheet says "Difficulty +2".</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Prefix { get; set; }

    /// <summary>How the host lays the badge out — <c>shrink-0</c> at the end of a flex row.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }
}
